findings.scenario:
The user copies Go or Makefile code whose first line is tab-indented, for example `	if err != nil {
		return err
	}`, and pastes it into the note, or into a ```go fence. The handler converts it to a table, so the code shows up as table cells with empty first columns. Any backslash in a cell is doubled, so the clipboard text `C:
ew` becomes `C:\new` in the note. The handler does not check whether the cursor is in a fenced block or in frontmatter. Cmd+Z restores the text, because the paste is a single transaction.
